From 481866b60f6d3dda52dc63a1fc7efeed1632835c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 20:39:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Finite strain behaviours given the displacement gradient

The new parameter DisplacementGradient of the behaviour integrators
gives the displacement gradient H = F - I instead of the deformation
gradient F to a finite strain behaviour (option gradient of the
FiniteStrainBehaviourOptions of MGIS). In single precision, F is only
known within about 6e-8, while H keeps the relative precision of small
strains: the behaviours written in the Green-Lagrange or Hencky strain
frameworks compute their strain from it without cancellation.

With this parameter:
- the gradients of the material are the displacement gradients, named
  DisplacementGradient, and start from zero;
- the macroscopic gradients are the macroscopic values of the gradients
  of the behaviour: H, which a caller may keep without rounding, and
  zero by default;
- the mechanical post-processings use F = I + H;
- small strain behaviours and the regularisations are refused, and so
  are periodic problems mixing behaviours given F and behaviours given
  H, whose macroscopic gradients differ.

Tests: uniaxial tension with H against the references computed with
F, for a Hencky behaviour (new) and a behaviour written with F; Cauchy
stress of the post-processing; in float, convergence of the Hencky
case with a relative tolerance of 1e-5, which F does not reach; the
refusals.
